{"assets":["/editorial-assets/914ff440b39587111e645d6989015b8fe1cd77390b4805854385f0b69a1869ce.png","/editorial-assets/27c2f1ea05df58af9a2f58ff2c2078b6b14ec9d8c6b95b2e9bef9e7b8acc5e4d.png","/editorial-assets/d84b0cd74ec8b001b98cff6677a918eb9f1d2e379aa3027f88568150a5179e23.png","/editorial-assets/d27ba5df84a8d2ff794115a8a44bb760bd893471ef63f3169e6c44b2723a5121.png"],"contestId":"14","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/14/problem/A"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A. Letter "}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"text","text":"To find the smallest rectangle containing the picture, iterate through the pairs (i,j) such that the j-th symbol in i-th line is '*'; find the minimum and maximum values of i and j from these pairs. The rectangle to output is ["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"min"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"max"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"] × ["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"min"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"max"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"]."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/14/problem/B"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B. Young Photographer "}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"text","text":"First we find the intersection of all segments. To do this, denote by m the rightmost of left ends of the segments and denote by M the leftmost of right ends of the segments. The intersection of the segments is [m,M] (or empty set if m>M). Now determine the nearest point from this segment. If "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" < "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", it's "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", and the answer is "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". If "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" > "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"M"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", it's "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"M"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", and the answer is "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"M"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". If "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/914ff440b39587111e645d6989015b8fe1cd77390b4805854385f0b69a1869ce.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":", it's "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", and the answer is 0. If m>M, then the answer is -1."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/14/problem/C"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"C. Four Segments "}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"text","text":"There must be many ways to solve this problem. The following one seems quite easy to code."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"First count the number of distinct points among segments' ends. If it's not equal to 4, the segments can't form a rectangle and we output \"NO\". Then calculate the minimum and maximum coordinates of the 4 points: "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"min"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"max"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"min"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"max"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". If "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"min"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"max"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" or "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"min"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"max"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", then even if the segments form a rectangle, it has zero area, so we also output \"NO\" in this case."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"Now if the segments indeed form a rectangle, we know the coordinates of its vertices — ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"min"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"min"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"), ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"min"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"max"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"), ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"max"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"min"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":") and ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"max"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"max"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"). We just check that every side of this rectangle appears in the input. If it is the case, we output \"YES\", otherwise we output \"NO\"."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/14/problem/D"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"D. Two Paths "}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"text","text":"Take any pair of non-intersecting paths. Since Flatland is connected, there must be a third path, connecting these two. Remove a road from the third path. Then Flatland is divided into two components — one containing the first path, and the other containing the second path. This observation suggests us the algorithm: iterate over the roads; for each road remove it, find the longest path in both connected components and multiply the lengths of these paths. The longest path in a tree can be found by depth first search from each leaf. "},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/14/problem/E"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"E. Camels "}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"text","text":"Let us call an index j such that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"  >  "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"  <  "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" a "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"cavity"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Also, we'll call humps and cavities by the common word "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"break"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Then there must be exactly "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"T"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1 breaks, and the first one must be a hump."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"Denote by "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"f"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"nth"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" the number of ways in which a camel with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" breaks, ending at the point (n,h), can be extended to the end (the vertical "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"N"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"N"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") so that the total number of breaks was equal to T. Note that:"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"1. "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"f"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"NTh"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 1, if h=1,2,3,4. (We have already finished the camel and it has T breaks)"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"2. "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"f"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Nth"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 0, if 0 ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" < "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"T"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"h"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 1, 2, 3, 4. (We have already finished the camel, but it has less than T breaks)"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"3. "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"f"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"T"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"h"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 0, if 1 ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"N"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"h"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 1, 2, 3, 4. (The camel has already more than T breaks)."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"Now we find the recurrent formula for "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"f"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"nth"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". Suppose that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is even. Then the last break was a cavity, and we are moving up currently. We can continue moving up, then the number of breaks stays the same, and we move to one of the points ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"h"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1), ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"h"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 2), ..., ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1, 4). Or we can move down, then the number of breaks increases by 1, and we move to one of the points ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"h"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1), ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"h"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 2), ..., ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1, 1). This gives us the formula"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/27c2f1ea05df58af9a2f58ff2c2078b6b14ec9d8c6b95b2e9bef9e7b8acc5e4d.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"If "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is odd, then the last break was a hump, and similar reasoning leads to the formula"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/d84b0cd74ec8b001b98cff6677a918eb9f1d2e379aa3027f88568150a5179e23.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"We can calculate "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"f"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"nth"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" by dynamic programming. Consider now the point (2, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"h"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") on a camel. There are h-1 ways to get to this point (starting from points (1, 1), ..., (1, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"h"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1)), and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"f"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2, 0, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"h"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" ways to extend the camel to the end. So the answer to the problem is "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/d27ba5df84a8d2ff794115a8a44bb760bd893471ef63f3169e6c44b2723a5121.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"document"},"schema":2,"sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/blog/entry/389"}